module dev.slatelang.slate

// `slate:window` in the JavaScript back end, which is ten refusals and no implementation.
//
// **A REFUSAL RATHER THAN AN `OWED` STUB, WHICH IS `slate:lmdb`'s CASE EXACTLY.** A name on the
// `OWED` list says *not built yet*, and that is a promise somebody is waiting on; this is a promise
// nobody could keep. A browser page **is** a web view already and cannot open a native window at all
// -- what it has is `window.open`, a second tab governed by a pop-up blocker, with no title bar to
// set, no size the page may insist on and no way to bind a function into itself. node has no window
// system in its standard library either: every desktop shell for it -- Electron, Tauri, node-gui --
// is a separate runtime rather than a module, so a program compiled by `slate js` and run anywhere
// would depend on something the tool cannot see and did not install.
//
// **So the sentence names the call, says why, and says where a window does work** -- which is the
// rule `abbrev`, `isDST` and `slate:lmdb`'s nineteen already follow. `docs/library/window.md` says
// the same thing where a reader is deciding whether to use this at all, which is before they have
// written the program that would draw the refusal.
//
// **AND THE WHOLE BLOCK IS BEHIND THE `webview` FEATURE, BECAUSE THE THREE SETS ARE CHECKED.**
// `tests_owed.sysl` asks that `$b`'s names and `builtin_names()` are the SAME set, so a refusal left
// here for a native this build does not register is a name in `$b` that nothing can reach -- which
// is the exact failure that check exists to catch, and it would fail honestly.
#if feature_webview
js_runtime_window() -> string = raw"""
        // -- window --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        // **`slate:window` is a NATIVE window**, and a JavaScript host has none to give. A page is
        // already inside one and cannot make another; node's desktop shells are separate runtimes
        // rather than modules. So this says so rather than sitting on `OWED`, which would be a
        // promise nobody can keep.
        const noWindow = (name) => () =>
            fault("`" + name + "` opens a native window, and no JavaScript host has one to open -- " +
                "a browser page is already inside a window and node has no window system, so this " +
                "module runs in the interpreter only")

        for (const name of ["window", "windowBind", "windowEval", "windowTitle", "windowSize",
            "windowNavigate", "windowHtml", "windowClose", "windowClosed", "windowDone"])
            B[name] = noWindow(name)
    """
#else
js_runtime_window() -> string = ""
#endif
